# Synopsis

Manage the tool sets of the current session, retrieve the full signature
of a tool, or explore the tool tree and tool documents.

# Tool sets

The tools of a session form three sets:

- The default tool set is loaded with full signatures when the session
  starts. It is configured by the user at session start and is usually
  small. No operation lists it alone by default: its tools are already
  fully visible to you.
- The extra tool set is loaded with stub signatures (a placeholder
  description and empty parameters): a tool whose description looks
  like a placeholder (such as "[deferred]") rather than a description
  of the tool carries a stub signature; call "describe" to retrieve
  its full signature before calling it.
- The enabled tool set is the union of the two, and is exactly the set
  of tools visible to you in the session: every tool you can see in
  your tool list is in either the default or the extra tool set, and a
  tool that is not in your tool list is in neither of them and can not
  be called, no matter what "list" reports. Unless the user asks, do
  not consider operating on tools outside the extra tool set.

# Operations

- "describe": return the full signature (description and parameters) of
  the tools of the extra tool set matching "pattern" (tool names or
  tree paths); with no "pattern", nothing is described.
  "whole_catalog" does not apply to "describe": tools outside the
  extra tool set can not be described directly.
- "list": list the tools of the extra tool set matching "pattern" (all
  of them when "pattern" is omitted); set "whole_catalog" to list
  the whole catalog instead, including tools that are not available in
  this session. Do not set "whole_catalog" on your own unless the
  user asks for it or the user's request needs it. Set "enabled" to
  list the enabled tool set instead (the default and the extra set
  together, i.e. what you can see in your tool list); "pattern" does
  not apply to it. "enabled" and "whole_catalog" can not be used
  together (an error). "with_summary" adds
  a short summary to each entry: the tool's own for a tool entry, the
  category's own for a directory entry. "start_index" and "count"
  select a page
  of the result: the page is clamped to the result (a "start_index"
  beyond "total" gives an empty page, a "count" beyond the rest gives
  only the rest), negative values are an error, and "total" is the
  size of the full result (request as many as it to obtain everything
  at once).
- "doc": read documents of a tool. Requires "toolname"; "docpaths"
  selects the documents to read (defaults to "README.md"; several
  documents can be read in one call). The paths are literal, not
  globs: pass the file paths reported by "listdoc" (not its directory
  entries). Documents that do not exist are silently omitted, and a
  "toolname" that names no installed tool is silently ignored: the
  result is then an empty "docs" list, not an error.
- "listdoc": list the documents of a tool. Requires "toolname";
  "pattern" can be used to filter by document path globs. Directory
  entries are listed with a trailing "/". A "toolname" that names no
  installed tool gives an empty list, not an error.
- "enable": add the tools matching "pattern" to the session's extra
  tool set; requires "pattern". Do not use it, and do not consider
  using it, unless the user explicitly asks for it. Tools that do not
  exist, or that are already in the enabled tool set, are ignored (an
  empty result, not an error).
- "disable": remove the tools matching "pattern" from the session's
  extra tool set; requires "pattern". Do not use it, and do not
  consider using it, unless the user explicitly asks for it. Tools
  that do not exist, or that are not in the extra tool set, are
  ignored (an empty result, not an error).

# Approval

"enable" and "disable" require the user's approval; in non-interactive
sessions they are rejected unless automatic approval is configured.
When the approval is rejected, the result is
{"rejected": <reason>}.

# Patterns

"pattern" is a list of space separated glob patterns; an entry matches
when it matches any of them. The glob semantics are those of POSIX
pathname expansion; non-POSIX extensions such as "**" are not
supported. The patterns match the whole catalog: patterns without "/"
match tool names of the whole catalog, while patterns containing "/"
match paths in the tool tree (e.g. "builtin/*"), for listing items
under the root of the tool tree, prefix your pattern with a "/" (e.g.
"/*"). Each operation then narrows the matches: "describe" and "list"
(without "whole_catalog") keep only the tools of the extra tool set,
"list" with "whole_catalog" keeps all matches, "enable" keeps the
tools not yet in the enabled tool set, and "disable" keeps the tools
of the extra tool set. For "listdoc" the pattern matches document
pathnames under the tool's doc directory. A word starting with "@"
names a tool preset (a named list of patterns, e.g. "@default"); it is
expanded in place and can be mixed with other words. A preset name
that does not exist is ignored.

# Tool tree

The tool tree is a classification tree over the available tools: directories
are categories and files are concrete tools (named after the tools). The
same tool may appear under multiple categories; each position is listed
separately. Directory entries are listed with a trailing "/", and a
pattern ending with "/" matches only directories. A file directly under
the tree root is a general-purpose tool that belongs to no category.

# Tool documents

Tool documents are plain-text (usually Markdown) files under the tool's
doc directory; they hold extended reference material for the model.
"README.md" is the conventional entry point.

# Returns

A JSON object. On success:
describe returns {"described":...,"tools":[{...}, ...]}; list returns
{"list":[{"name" or "path":..., "summary"?:...},...],"total":...,
"start_index":...,"count":...}; doc returns
{"toolname":...,"docs":[{"docpath":...,"content":...},...]}; listdoc returns
{"toolname":...,"doclist":[...]}; enable returns
{"added_tools":[...],"count":...}; disable returns
{"removed_tools":[...],"count":...}.
On failure: {"error": <reason>}; when the user rejects the approval
request of "enable" or "disable": {"rejected": <reason>}.

# Notes

- Parameters that do not apply to the requested operation are silently
  ignored.
- The read-only operations (describe, list, doc, listdoc) can be
  issued in parallel; "enable" and "disable" must run one at a time.
- The tool sets are scoped to the current session.
- Use this tool to inspect what is available and "describe" to learn how
  to call a tool; use the shell tool to read other files.
- The tool document "README.md" holds the interface details and result
  examples; read it with "doc" when they are needed.
